 char` to `int` so that `d[3] << 24` becomes negative and is then SIGN-EXTENDED when widened to size_t (this happens in stbds_siphash_bytes both in the main 8-byte loop and in the tail switch cases 4/3/2/1).\n3. Pointer arithmetic: element offsets (elemsize*i + keyoffset), the -1/+1 header offsets, array-base vs hash-pointer (`a` vs `raw_a`) usage in every statement of stbds_hmput_key / stbds_hmdel_key / stbds_hmfree_func.\n4. Struct field layout/order and sizes used in allocation size computations.\n5. Which values are written to table->temp_key (stbds_temp_key) and stbds_header()->temp (stbds_temp), and in which branches (note: the C sets temp_key in ONE of the two inner probe loops of stbds_hmput_key but not the other — the Rust must copy that asymmetry).\n6. Loop bounds and the goto/label control flow (`goto done`, `goto found_empty_slot`) translated faithfully.\n7. Constants: 0x31415926 initial seed, the stbds_load_32_or_64 derived multiplier/addend, siphash constants, bucket length 8, cache line 64, arena block sizes 512 / (1<<20), STBDS_HASH_DELETED=1, STBDS_INDEX_DELETED=-2.\n\nReport ONLY concrete discrepancies, each as: C line number(s), Rust line number(s), what differs, and whether it changes observable behavior. If a function is faithful, say so in one short line. Be precise and skeptical; do not speculate about things you did not read.","agentId":"a5b0f2d975e98b549","agentType":"general-purpose","harnessNoteCount":0,"harnessTailCount":0,"harnessSectionHash":"70a929250fc5205c","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I read both files in full and compared every listed function statement-by-statement. Here is the result.\n\n## Findings\n\n**No behavioral discrepancies found.** Every function in the list is a faithful translation. Details below, plus a handful of non-behavioral deviations worth knowing about.\n\n### Function-by-function verdict (all faithful)\n\n| C | Rust | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| `stbds_arrgrowf` (c 276-310) | rs 280-321 | Faithful. `min_len` uses `ptrdiff_t→size_t` conversion, `2*arrcap` in `size_t`, realloc size `elemsize*min_cap + 32`, `+32` re-base, `a==NULL` init of length/hash_table/temp, then unconditional `capacity=min_cap`. |\n| `stbds_arrfreef` (312-315) | 324-326 | Faithful (frees `a-32`, no null check, same as C). |\n| `stbds_rand_seed` (355-358) | 345-347 | Faithful. |\n| `stbds_hash_string` (477-491) | 520-536 | Faithful, including the two no-op `hash ^= hash ^ ROTATE_RIGHT(...)` idioms (which reduce to plain assignment in both), `(~hash)+(hash<<18)` using the pre-assignment `hash`, `*21`, `+= (hash<<6)`, final `+seed`. |\n| `stbds_siphash_bytes` (498-551) | 559-642 | Faithful — see sign-extension notes below. |\n| `stbds_hash_bytes` (553-556) | 645-647 | Faithful. |\n| `stbds_hmfree_func` (571-584) | 675-691 | Faithful. `a` is the raw array base in both; loop starts at `i=1`, re-reads `header(a)->length` each iteration, `strreset(&table->string)`, then frees `hash_table` then `header(a)`. |\n| `stbds_hmget_key_ts` (631-657) | 769-801 | Faithful, including the `length += 1` **before** `memset` ordering in the NULL branch, `*temp=-1` for the null-table case vs `STBDS_INDEX_EMPTY` for `slot<0`, and returning `a` (not `ARR_TO_HASH(a)`) in the else branch. |\n| `stbds_hmget_key` (659-665) | 804-815 | Faithful. |\n| `stbds_hmput_default` (667-676) | 818-836 | Faithful, including `length += 1` **before** `memset` (opposite of `hmput_key`). |\n| `stbds_hmput_key` (680-794) | 846-1030 | Faithful — see detailed notes below. |\n| `stbds_shmode_func` (796-805) | 1033-1041 | Faithful (`length = 1` assignment, not `+= 1`; `mode as u8` truncation). |\n| `stbds_hmdel_key` (807-868) | 1044-1132 | Faithful — see detailed notes below. |\n| `stbds_stralloc` (881-918) | 1146-1196 | Faithful. `512 << (block>>1)`, `++a->block` only when `blocksize < 1<<20`, oversized-block path splices behind `a->storage` without touching `remaining`, `sizeof(*sb)-8 == 8`. |\n| `stbds_strreset` (920-930) | 1199-1209 | Faithful (`memset` of 24 bytes). |\n| `strkey` (939-943) | 1219-1257 | Faithful; the hand-rolled `%d` formatter matches `printf(\"%d\")` for the whole `int` range including `INT_MIN`, and writes into a private 256-byte static. |\n| `arr_push` (945-956) | 1260-1291 | Faithful expansion of `arrmaybegrow`/`arrput`/`arrfree` with `elemsize == 4`, `i += 50` outer step. |\n| `stbds_make_hash_index` (385-472) | 371-503 | Faithful — see notes. |\n| `stbds_probe_position` (367-373) | 357-359 | Faithful. |\n| `stbds_log2` (375-383) | 361-369 | Faithful. |\n| `stbds_is_key_equal` (558-564) | 653-672 | Faithful (`mode >= STBDS_HM_STRING` → strcmp on `*(char**)(a+elemsize*i+keyoffset)`, else memcmp of `keysize`). |\n| `stbds_hm_find_slot` (586-629) | 693-766 | Faithful, both probe sub-loops, `hash<2 → hash+=2` before `probe_position`, `(pos & ~7)+i` return. |\n| `stbds_strdup` (870-876) | 838-843 | Faithful. |\n| Macros `stbds_header`/`temp`/`temp_key`/`arrlen`/`arrcap`/`HASH_TO_ARR`/`ARR_TO_HASH`/`ALIGN_FWD`/`ROTATE_LEFT`/`ROTATE_RIGHT`/`load_32_or_64` | 213-273, 353-359, 510-517, 412-433 | All faithful. |\n\n### Specifically verified high-risk items\n\n**Sign extension in `stbds_siphash_bytes`.** All four sites are correct:\n- Main loop low word (c 523 / rs 578-582): C's `d[0]|(d[1]<<8)|(d[2]<<16)|(d[3]<<24)` is `int`-typed and sign-extends into `size_t`. Rust builds a `u32` then `((lo as i32) as isize) as usize` — exact match.\n- Main loop high word (c 524 / rs 583-587): the C cast `(size_t)(...)` happens **before** `<<16<<16`, so the sign-extended 64-bit value is shifted. Rust does `((((hi as i32) as isize) as usize) << 16) << 16` — cast-then-shift order preserved.\n- Tail `case 4` (c 536 / rs 614): `(d[3] << 24)` sign-extends. Rust `((*d.add(3) as i32).wrapping_shl(24)) as isize as usize` — correct.\n- Tail `cases 3/2/1` (c 537-539 / rs 617-623): values stay positive; Rust routes them through `i32 → isize → usize` which is a no-op. Correct.\n\n**Switch fall-through in the siphash tail** (c 532-541 / rs 602-625): the C `switch(len-i)` with no `break`s is rendered as a cascade of `if rem >= 7 { } if rem >= 6 { } …`, which reproduces the fall-through exactly for every `rem` in 0..=7. `case 0: break` maps to no work. `data = len << 56` is set before the cascade in both.\n\n**`stbds_temp_key` asymmetry in `stbds_hmput_key`.** Confirmed present in the Rust: the first inner probe loop (c 731-734 / rs 924-933) sets `stbds_temp(a)` **and**, when `mode >= STBDS_HM_STRING`, `stbds_temp_key(a) = *(char**)(raw_a + elemsize*index + keyoffset)`; the second inner loop (c 749-750 / rs 961-962) sets only `stbds_temp(a)`. The asymmetry is copied.\n\n**`a` vs `raw_a` usage.** In `stbds_hmput_key` the C reuses the names confusingly (`raw_a` holds the *hash* pointer, `a` the raw base after line 694). The Rust reproduces this exactly at rs 866-867, and every downstream use matches: `stbds_is_key_equal(raw_a, …)` (c 730/748 → rs 915/952), `temp_key` source from `raw_a` (c 733 → rs 926-931), `stbds_temp(a)`/`header(a)->length`/element writes from `a` (c 731/779/786-789 → rs 924/996/1002), returns `STBDS_ARR_TO_HASH(a,elemsize)`. In `stbds_hmdel_key` the roles are reversed (`raw_a` = raw base, `a` = hash pointer) and the Rust matches: `header(raw_a)->{temp,length,hash_table}` and `arrlen(raw_a)`, but all element/`elemsize*index` arithmetic off `a` (rs 1057, 1069, 1078, 1083-1084, 1092, 1102, 1114, 1119, 1123).\n\n**goto → labeled break.** `goto done` (c 447/454/465) → `break 'probe` out of the `for(;;)` so control resumes at `++j` (rs 476/486, `j += 1` at 496). `goto found_empty_slot` (c 738/754/765) → `break 'search` out of the `loop` so control resumes at the tombstone fixup (rs 938/966 → 981). Both correct, and `pos = (pos & ~7)+i` is assigned before the break in both sites.\n\n**`stbds_load_32_or_64` constants.** The C constant types matter: `0x87b0b0fd`/`0xb504f32d` are `unsigned int`, `2147001325`/`715136305` are `int`, so `v64_lo ^ v32` is a 32-bit unsigned XOR zero-extended into `size_t`. The Rust does `(0x87b0b0fdu32 ^ 2147001325u32) as usize` (rs 414, 424) — same. Net derived values: `a = 0x27bb2ee687b0b0fd`, `b = 0xb504f32d`, and `hash_seed = hash_seed * a + b` is applied **after** `t->seed = stbds_hash_seed` (c 409/412 → rs 411/433).\n\n**Struct layout / allocation sizes.** `stbds_hash_index` field order matches the C exactly (temp_key, slot_count, used_count, used_count_threshold, used_count_shrink_threshold, tombstone_count, tombstone_count_threshold, seed, slot_count_log2, string, storage) and the `const _: () = assert!(...)` checks at rs 175-183 pin sizes 32/16/24/128/104 and offsets 72/96/16/17, which are the correct x86-64 SysV values. Allocation size `(slot_count>>3)*128 + 104 + 63` matches c 388, and `t+1` → `+104` matches c 389.\n\n**Other constants.** Seed `0x31415926` (rs 332), `STBDS_BUCKET_LENGTH 8` / `SHIFT 3` / `MASK 7` (rs 146-148), `STBDS_CACHE_LINE_SIZE 64` (rs 149), arena `512` / `1<<20` (rs 1138-1139), `STBDS_HASH_DELETED 1` (rs 200), `STBDS_INDEX_DELETED -2` (rs 197), siphash init words and `0x0706050403020100`/`0x0f0e0d0c0b0a0908`/`^0xff`/2 C-rounds/4 D-rounds — all match.\n\n**`mode` comparison asymmetry.** `stbds_hm_find_slot`, `stbds_is_key_equal`, `stbds_hmput_key` use `mode >= STBDS_HM_STRING`, but `stbds_hmdel_key` uses `mode == STBDS_HM_STRING` (c 836, 842). Rust preserves the `==` at rs 1077 and 1088.\n\n### Non-behavioral deviations (for completeness, not defects)\n\n1. **`stbds_assert` is a no-op** (rs 115). The default CMake build of the C has `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=` (empty) and no `-DNDEBUG`, so `assert()` is **live** in the C `.so`. On an invariant violation the C would `abort()` while the Rust silently continues. All six asserts are true invariants under valid API use (e.g. `used_count_threshold + tombstone_count_threshold < slot_count` holds for every power-of-two `slot_count >= 8`), and `STBDS_ASSERT(table->used_count >= 0)` at c 832 is vacuously true for `size_t` and is simply absent from the Rust. Not observable for correct usage, but it is the one place C and Rust could diverge.\n2. `stbds_arrgrowf`'s unused local `stbds_array_header temp={0}` / `(void)sizeof(temp)` (c 278, 281) is omitted — no effect.\n3. All `STBDS_STATS(...)` sites are omitted; they expand to nothing in the C (c 271).\n4. `stbds_strdup` (rs 839) and `stbds_stralloc` (rs 1151) use non-wrapping `c_strlen(s) + 1`; the C wraps. Only reachable for a `SIZE_MAX`-length string — unreachable.\n5. The dead `raw_a = STBDS_ARR_TO_HASH(a,elemsize)` store (c 776) is preserved with `let _ = raw_a;` (rs 992-993); the trailing `let _ = STBDS_SH_NONE; let _ = STBDS_HM_BINARY;` in `arr_push` (rs 1289-1290) are dead-code-warning suppressors. No effect.\n6. `stbds_stralloc` uses `wrapping_shl` (rs 1155) where C has `512 << (block>>1)`. A shift count `>= 64` would be UB in C; `wrapping_shl` masks to 6 bits, matching what x86-64 `shlq` does. In practice `a->block` never exceeds 22, so the shift count never reaches 32, let alone 64.\n\n### Corroboration\n\nThe pre-existing harness artifacts in `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-4nlDhF/_test/` (a 35 KB `harness.c` exercising all 16 exported symbols across ~373 KB of output) show byte-identical output between the Rust `.so` (release and debug) and the C `.so` built at default, `-O0`, and `-O3`: `out_rs.txt == out_rs_dbg.txt == out_cbuild.txt == out_cb_default.txt == out_cb_O0.txt == out_cb_O3.txt`. I did not rebuild or modify anything."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":495243,"totalTokens":106204,"totalToolUseCount":12,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8591,"cache_read_input_tokens":90854,"output_tokens":6757,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1927},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8591},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed"